#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877} :activity/id #uuid "ea872253-9665-5737-9ff4-e1c4c5546bda" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120877 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121122 :tutorial/content "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = 0,$ then we say that the two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are **linearly dependent**.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider two sets of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\n\\textrm{I.}\\:\\: \\Bigg\\{ \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}, \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{a}_2} \\Bigg\\}\n}\n{\\color{red}\n\\qquad\\qquad\n\\textrm{II.}\\:\\: \\Bigg\\{ \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{b}_1}, \\underbrace{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}}_{\\mathbf{b}_2} \\Bigg\\}\n}\n$$\n\n- For the first set ${\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\color{blue}{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{1}{\\color{blue}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}+{x}_{2}{\\color{blue}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is true only if $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ is *linearly independent*.\n\n- On the other hand, for the second set ${\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{b}}_{1}}+{x}_{2}{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{b}}_{2}} & =\\mathbf{0} \\\\ {x}_{1}{\\color{red}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}+{x}_{2}{\\color{red}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has solutions other than $x_1=x_2=0.$ For example, $x_1=2$ and $x_2=1$ gives\n$$\n2 \\cdot {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}} + 1 \\cdot {\\color{red}{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ - 2\\end{array} \\right]}} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\nTherefore, the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ is *not* linearly independent. Rather, it is *linearly dependent*.\n\n**Note:** You may notice that the vectors in the set $\\color{red}(\\textrm{II})$ were parallel (i.e. multiples of one another), while the vectors in set $\\color{blue}(\\textrm{I})$ were not. This trend holds in general, for all sets of two vectors:\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel, while\n\n- a set of two vectors is *linearly independent* if and only if the two vectors are not parallel." :tutorial/id #uuid "938aba59-31f8-5ad9-8d3d-ce9287feb3de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6811 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b84b116-3328-5055-9d5b-02360cff09e6" :step/math-academy-id 41352 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121123 :tutorial/content "Any set of two vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. To see this, consider the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.$ We can realize that this set is linearly dependent using either of the following two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v} + x_2 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1=0$ and $x_2=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ So, the set is linearly dependent.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, recall that a set of two vectors is linearly dependent if and only if the two vectors are parallel. That is, the two vectors are multiples of each other.\n\nThe zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to every other vector because it is a multiple of any other vector. For any vector $\\mathbf{v},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{0} = 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v} \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\mathbf{0} \\parallel \\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nSo, because $\\mathbf{0}$ is parallel to $\\mathbf{v},$ the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}$ is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "ef2c96dd-d1cd-593e-a998-ba1be6c42686" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6812 :tutorial/title "Sets of Two Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "e814b4bf-688c-57df-8849-f4f2455723dd" :step/math-academy-id 41353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120879 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119497 :question/id #uuid "cbabcd64-6a33-552d-9f2f-704a9df44d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10906" :question/problem "The set $\\{ \\mathbf{a_1},\\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n$$\n\\mathbf{a_1} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 21\\end{array} \\right]}?\n$$\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = \\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nTo see why, notice that \n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}= \\dfrac 1 3 {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}12 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 21\\end{array} \\right]} = \\dfrac 1 3\\mathbf{a}_1.\n$$\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{2} & =\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1  +x_2  \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=\\dfrac 1 3$ and $x_2=-1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=\\mathbf{0}.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048315}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705858} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120321} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705857} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705903} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186705920}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"} :step/id #uuid "efae78f1-ab53-50ee-aa00-035536697ae6" :step/math-academy-id 41354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121124 :tutorial/content "So far, we've discussed linear dependence and linear independence for sets of two vectors. However, these concepts can be extended to sets of any number of vectors.\n\nA set of $n$ vectors $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is called **linearly independent** if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_n \\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1=x_2=\\ldots=x_n=0.$ Otherwise, if there exists any solution other than $x_1 = x_2 = \\ldots = x_n = 0,$ then we say that the set $\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\right\\}$ is **linearly dependent**.\n\nSimilar to sets containing two vectors, a general set of $n$ vectors is dependent if *any* two vectors in the set are parallel.\n\n**But be careful!** When working with sets containing more than two vectors, having a linearly dependent set is possible even if *none* of the vectors are parallel! \n\nFor example, consider the following set of vectors: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}\n\t\\right\\}\n$$\n\nNone of the vectors in this set are parallel. However, we have\n\n$$\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} + {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} - {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]} + x_2 \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}+ x_3 \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nhas a solution $x_1 = 1,$ $x_2 = 1,$ and $x_3 = -1,$ which is different from $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that the vectors are linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d550a61-4219-5ffc-83a2-155ddd8fa9f4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6813 :tutorial/title "Linear Dependence and Independence for a General Set of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "46556bbf-0804-5a49-af3c-3a10e60ee378" :step/math-academy-id 41357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121125 :tutorial/content "As with sets containing only two vectors, any set of vectors that contains the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}$ must be linearly dependent. \n\nTo see this, consider the set \n\n$$\n\\left\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_{n-1}, \\mathbf{0} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\ldots + x_{n-1} \\mathbf{v}_{n-1} + x_n \\cdot \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_{n-1}=0$ and $x_n=1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=\\ldots = x_n=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." :tutorial/id #uuid "7270de59-1d83-5820-aa64-a5f4ca262e3e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6814 :tutorial/title "General Sets of Vectors Containing the Zero Vector"} :step/id #uuid "50295cf6-d08a-5be4-b71d-2116227de4f2" :step/math-academy-id 41358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119498 :question/id #uuid "7b28b42f-0ac4-543e-baeb-51534aaad480" :question/math-academy-id "e-10907" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]},\\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 7\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nII. $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nIII. The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{a}}_{1}-{\\mathbf{a}}_{2}-2{\\mathbf{a}}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne \\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 7{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0} \\\\ 7{x}_{3} &=\\mathbf{0}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707105} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275381} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275382} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275385} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275386} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275387} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707084} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707100}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "8247f0d4-142e-58a3-b2ed-d612accf2a6c" :step/math-academy-id 41359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119925 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119499 :question/id #uuid "3843dc13-0908-5d4c-adfe-836a928a1410" :question/math-academy-id "e-10908" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{a}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2,$ and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\nII. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n\nIII. The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}+{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ while $x_3$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707014} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317320} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186433173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186564487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186706986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707030} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186707050}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "4922cdd4-be71-550d-b216-e5a30b232248" :step/math-academy-id 41362}] :activity/title "Linear Dependence and Independence" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120052}]}
